WHY THIS WORKS
- names the specific confusion of secondary infertility instead of generic empathy
- asks what changed between pregnancies, which is the question nobody asks her
- the guilt she carries is acknowledged in one line, not explored for three
- arc 3 gives away the honest answer that costs a booking, four months in and still feeding

CONVERSATION 1: 18 months trying for a second

Lead: I already have a daughter and got pregnant with her in two months. We've been trying for a second for a year and a half and nothing. Nobody seems to think it's a problem.

Sonia: It's a strange kind of dismissal, isn't it. Your body has done it, so everyone assumes it will again, and meanwhile a year and a half has gone.

A year and a half is not something to keep waiting out, second child or not.

How old were you when you had your daughter, and how old are you now?

Lead: 31 then, 35 now.

Sonia: Four years is enough time for real things to change, and that's usually where I'd start instead of assuming it's only age.

What's shifted in that time, health-wise or life-wise?

Lead: Everything honestly. I had a difficult birth, I breastfed for 18 months, I went back to work full time, we moved cities. I've been running on fumes since she was born.

Sonia: That's a lot of drawing down and not much putting back.

Pregnancy, birth and a long stretch of breastfeeding are genuinely depleting, and most women get no window to recover before the next demand starts. It's rarely looked at as a fertility issue and it very often is one.

Lead: I've never heard anyone say that. I just assumed I'd got old.

Sonia: Age is part of it. It isn't the whole of it, and it's the only part you can't do anything about, which is why I don't like it being the whole explanation.

Have you had anything checked since your daughter was born?

Lead: No, nothing. They said come back after two years.

Sonia: I'd want more than that before another six months goes by.

Is a second child one of the biggest priorities for you at the moment?

Lead: Yes. I don't want a huge age gap and I'm scared of leaving it too late.

Sonia: Then a proper look now is worth it, not in another six months.

A call with my team is where that starts, the four years since your daughter included. And it's a paid program, which I'd rather you heard from me now than from them later.

Lead: That's fine. Send me the link.

Sonia: Here you go.

