CONVERSATION 2: carrying guilt about wanting a second

Lead: is it selfish to be this upset about not having a second when I already have one? my sister has none.

Sonia: No, and being grateful for the child you have doesn't cancel out wanting another.

Those two things sit alongside each other for most women in your position. The guilt is common and it isn't accurate.

Lead: it makes it hard to even talk about it

Sonia: I know. Secondary infertility tends to get you the least sympathy of any version of this, which is why women in it so often go quiet.

How long have you been trying?

Lead: two years next month.

Sonia: That's long enough that I wouldn't accept "you've already got one" as a reason not to investigate.

Has anything been looked at in that time?

Lead: not really. I've been embarrassed to push.

Sonia: You're allowed to push. It's a real thing with real causes and you're two years in.

If you want, I'll have my team go through the whole picture with you on a call and tell you where I'd actually start.

It's paid coaching, so you know that going in rather than finding out halfway through the hour.

Lead: yes, I'd like that. and that's fine.

Sonia: Grab a time that works.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 3: four months trying, still breastfeeding, told honestly she does not need this yet

Lead: 33, trying for number two since january so about 4 months. still feeding my son twice a day. should I be worried? do I need a program

Sonia: Four months, and still feeding twice a day. No, I don't think you need me.

Breastfeeding suppresses ovulation to different degrees in different women, and twice a day is enough to be part of the picture at four months.

Lead: so should I stop feeding?

Sonia: That's your call and not one I'd make for you. Plenty of women conceive while still feeding. If your cycles have come back and are reasonably regular, I'd give it more time before changing anything you're not ready to change.

Lead: they came back in november and they're pretty regular, 29 or 30 days

Sonia: Then that's a good sign and four months is well within normal, even when it doesn't feel it.

Lead: everyone said it would happen straight away like last time

Sonia: It often doesn't, and that comparison is the thing that makes four months feel like two years.

Come back to me if you get to a year, or sooner if your cycles go strange or something specific worries you. I'd rather see you then with a real question than take you on now for something time is likely to sort.

Lead: that's really reassuring, thank you

Sonia: Watch my masterclass in the meantime if you want something useful to do with the worry.

